   "text": "# The History of the Printing Press: A Revolution in Human Communication\n\nFew inventions in human history have reshaped civilization as profoundly as the printing press. The ability to reproduce text mechanically\u2014rapidly, cheaply, and in large quantities\u2014transformed religion, politics, science, education, and culture across the globe. What follows is a detailed account of the printing press's history, from its distant precursors in Asia and the medieval manuscript tradition, through Gutenberg's epochal innovation in fifteenth-century Europe, to its spread, its social consequences, and its long evolution into the industrial machines of the nineteenth century and beyond.\n\n## Precursors: Woodblock Printing and Movable Type in Asia\n\nLong before the European printing press emerged, several societies developed sophisticated methods for reproducing text and images. In China, woodblock printing\u2014carving a page's content in relief on a wooden block, inking it, and pressing paper against it\u2014was in use by the seventh century during the Tang dynasty. The earliest surviving dated printed book, a copy of the Diamond Sutra from 868 CE, was produced this way. Woodblock printing allowed Buddhist scriptures, calendars, and administrative documents to be reproduced in quantities that manuscript copying could never match, and by the Song dynasty, China possessed",
