   "text": "# The Press That Remade the World: A History of Printing\n\nFew inventions in human history have exerted as profound and far-reaching an influence as the printing press. Before its advent, knowledge in Europe was largely the preserve of a small elite\u2014monasteries, courts, and wealthy merchants\u2014while the vast majority of people lived in a world of oral tradition and hand-copied manuscripts. The emergence of mechanized movable-type printing in the mid-fifteenth century shattered that monopoly, setting in motion transformations that would reshape religion, science, politics, education, and culture across the globe. To trace the history of the printing press is to trace the history of modernity itself: the Reformation, the Scientific Revolution, the Enlightenment, and the rise of democratic politics all depended, to a remarkable degree, on the cheap and rapid dissemination of the written word. This essay examines the long prehistory of printing, the invention and spread of the press, its social and intellectual consequences, the regulatory struggles it provoked, and its eventual globalization, while also considering the deeper questions it raises about technology, authority, and access to knowledge.\n\n## Precursors: Writing, Paper, and Early Reproduction Technologies\n\nThe story of printing begins long before Gutenberg. Every civilization that developed writing faced the problem of",
